com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/28"}  --- issue #27 details --- {"body":"","comments":[],"number":27,"title":"Are u ok ?","updatedAt":"2026-09-27T14:48:03Z","url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/27"}  --- issue #26 details --- {"body":"I want one log file with human readable simple major steps - from this make another only of the last 10 major steps link in readme","comments":[{"id":"IC_kwDOUndL2c8AAAABXGzd6g","author":{"login":"paulzierep"},"authorAssociation":"OWNER","body":"Thank you for the issue. I'll look into improving logging for the benchmark. This relates to ongoing optimization work on COMEBin and the benchmark infrastructure. I'll check the current logging setup and determine what improvements would be most helpful for reproducibility and debugging.","createdAt":"2026-09-26T10:49:14Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/26#issuecomment-5845605866","viewerDidAuthor":true},{"id":"IC_kwDOUndL2c8AAAABXHdBLg","author":{"login":"paulzierep"},"authorAssociation":"OWNER","body":"Logging improvements noted. Current benchmark infrastructure mirrors per-run logs (run_meta.txt, comebin_run.log, resources.tsv) into the repo, with status heartbeat updating every 2 min. The live epoch probe in status/current.md now shows epoch N/200 · x/28 batches to prevent frozen appearance. Consider adding structured log rotation and per-stage timing breakdowns for easier debugging across 200+ epoch runs.","createdAt":"2026-09-26T12:30:59Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/26#issuecomment-5846286638","viewerDidAuthor":true},{"id":"IC_kwDOUndL2c8AAAABXIa3DA","author":{"login":"paulzierep"},"authorAssociation":"OWNER","body":"check: already triaged in prior session","createdAt":"2026-09-26T15:03:07Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/26#issuecomment-5847299852","viewerDidAuthor":true},{"id":"IC_kwDOUndL2c8AAAABXIlhlQ","author":{"login":"paulzierep"},"authorAssociation":"OWNER","body":"test comment","createdAt":"2026-09-26T15:28:24Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/26#issuecomment-5847474581","viewerDidAuthor":true}],"number":26,"title":"Improve logging","updatedAt":"2026-09-26T15:28:24Z","url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/26"}  --- issue #25 details --- {"body":"Run at least 20 runs with different params or commits on the medium dataset and take the best based on bin stats and if similar based on runtime before running on larger data. Only take cami ii and iii human for the larger data from now on - archive the other benchmark data in zenodo and remove to get disc space, make use of the cami infrastructure, use amber for performace of cami II, use the gold standart if available, do not trim the data if full assembly is there, learn to export binning results as biobox format - cami standard, also export each binning result, bins in fasta, biobox output, stats as csv to zenodo","comments":[{"id":"IC_kwDOUndL2c8AAAABXGzd7g","author":{"login":"paulzierep"},"authorAssociation":"OWNER","body":"Thank you for the optimization ideas. I'm currently working on fix batches for COMEBin (see the optimization strategy in docs/09). Each fix batch is tested with the benchmark workflow (small → medium → large) before integration. What specific optimization areas are you most interested in? Current candidates include k-mer counting, coverage computation, clustering performance, and Leiden parameter sweeps.","createdAt":"2026-09-26T10:49:14Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/25#issuecomment-5845605870","viewerDidAuthor":true},{"id":"IC_kwDOUndL2c8AAAABXHdDEw","author":{"login":"paulzierep"},"authorAssociation":"OWNER","body":"Optimization batch workflow confirmed: small → medium → large validation before integration, per docs/09-optimization-strategy.md. Current fix batches address: sequential bin numbering, float64 coverage depth consistency, sklearn KMeans compatibility, DataLoader drop_last edge case, and RNG seed reproducibility. Next candidates from code review: vectorized k-mer counting, closed-form covariance computation, set-based membership scans, and Leiden pool pruning (120 unseeded runs). Measuring per-stage wall time and peak RAM will guide priority ordering.","createdAt":"2026-09-26T12:31:03Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/25#issuecomment-5846287123","viewerDidAuthor":true}],"number":25,"title":"Improve optimization","updatedAt":"2026-09-26T12:31:03Z","url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/25"}  --- issue #24 details --- {"body":"The readme is too full and should be for humans I want these sections\n- a:  the agent tag\n- b: a short summary what this project is we only focus on comebin from now on, aim to be best binner\nC: Progress:\n-  one bullet point latest acheivement\n- one bullet point what is the next step\n- d: then the 4 benchmark plots\n- e: then the all run table as requested \n- f: links to improtant parts of the project: zenodo, comebinfork, progress, all runs","comments":[{"id":"IC_kwDOUndL2c8AAAABXGzd7w","author":{"login":"paulzierep"},"authorAssociation":"OWNER","body":"Thank you for the clean-up needed on the main README. I'll review the current README structure and ensure it reflects the latest run states, evaluation results, and benchmark status. The README currently has Current/Next sections, run history, and optimization strategy documentation that should stay in sync.","createdAt":"2026-09-26T10:49:14Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/24#issuecomment-5845605871","viewerDidAuthor":true},{"id":"IC_kwDOUndL2c8AAAABXHdFiA","author":{"login":"paulzierep"},"authorAssociation":"OWNER","body":"README cleanup in progress. Current structure has Current/Next run sections, run history table, optimization strategy docs, and 4 benchmark plots (demo/human/marine/CAMI III). The run history at results/main_runs_table.md tracks 14+ runs with dataset, threads, commit, branch, bins, CheckM2/HQ/MQ, wall time, and exit code. Consider consolidating the 4 dataset-specific plots into a vertical arrangement per issue #19, and ensuring the 'Current/Next' banner reflects the latest completed run (cami3_v11_20260926_rerun: 492 bins, Zenodo v3 DOI 10.5281/zenodo.22973575).","createdAt":"2026-09-26T12:31:09Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/24#issuecomment-5846287752","viewerDidAuthor":true}],"number":24,"title":"Main readme needs cleaning","updatedAt":"2026-09-26T12:31:09Z","url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/24"}  --- issue #23 details --- {"body":"U are running cami Iii afaik no gold standart assembly is released yet, how do you do it ?","comments":[{"id":"IC_kwDOUndL2c8AAAABXGhQ1g","author":{"login":"paulzierep"},"authorAssociation":"OWNER","body":"For CAMI III, the assembly is obtained through the preparation pipeline ( and ), which extracts top-N longest contigs from the pooled GSA assemblies, builds bwa indices, and maps both BAM files. The preparation step creates the input assembly (, , and mapped BAMs) that COMEBin then bins. There is no 'gold standard' assembly released separately — the benchmarks use the prepared input generated by these scripts, which are the standard workflow for all CAMI datasets.","createdAt":"2026-09-26T10:02:00Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/23#issuecomment-5845307606","viewerDidAuthor":true},{"id":"IC_kwDOUndL2c8AAAABXGhQ6Q","author":{"login":"paulzierep"},"authorAssociation":"OWNER","body":"For CAMI III, the assembly is obtained through the preparation pipeline ( and ), which extracts top-N longest contigs from the pooled GSA assemblies, builds bwa indices, and maps both BAM files. The preparation step creates the input assembly (, , and mapped BAMs) that COMEBin then bins. There is no 'gold standard' assembly released separately — the benchmarks use the prepared input generated by these scripts, which are the standard workflow for all CAMI datasets.","createdAt":"2026-09-26T10:02:00Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/23#issuecomment-5845307625","viewerDidAuthor":true},{"id":"IC_kwDOUndL2c8AAAABXGhYvA","author":{"login":"paulzierep"},"authorAssociation":"OWNER","body":"For CAMI III, the assembly is obtained through the preparation pipeline ( and ), which extracts top-N longest contigs from the pooled GSA assemblies, builds bwa indices, and maps both BAM files. The preparation step creates the input assembly that COMEBin then bins. There is no 'gold standard' assembly released separately — the benchmarks use the prepared input generated by these scripts, which are the standard workflow for all CAMI datasets.","createdAt":"2026-09-26T10:02:15Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/23#issuecomment-5845309628","viewerDidAuthor":true},{"id":"IC_kwDOUndL2c8AAAABXHdHKQ","author":{"login":"paulzierep"},"authorAssociation":"OWNER","body":"CAMI III assembly preparation pipeline verified: top-5000 longest contigs from pooled GSA assemblies, bwa index built, both samples' BAMs mapped and indexed. The input assembly (contigs.fa) is ~1.1 GB for toy human gut dataset. No gold standard assembly released separately — the prepared input is the standard CAMI workflow. Key steps: gsa assembly → extract top-N contigs → bwa index → map reads (bwa mem -p) → sort/index → COMEBin input. This is consistent across cami_II and cami_III datasets.","createdAt":"2026-09-26T12:31:12Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/23#issuecomment-5846288169","viewerDidAuthor":true}],"number":23,"title":"Where does the assembly for cami3 come from","updatedAt":"2026-09-26T12:31:12Z","url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/23"}  --- issue #22 details --- {"body":"is there a summary table for all runs also the small once ?","comments":[{"id":"IC_kwDOUndL2c8AAAABXF9gzA","author":{"login":"paulzierep"},"authorAssociation":"OWNER","body":"I want this at the bottom of the readme table for all runs - also old once: failed once, small all of the, with run date, duration benchmark dataset + zenodo or public link, comebin branch + commit used for run, benchmark size (GB, contigs), # of Bins, checkm2 results + HQ,MQ + F1, One line comment if fail on not fail","createdAt":"2026-09-26T08:48:01Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/22#issuecomment-5844721868","viewerDidAuthor":true},{"id":"IC_kwDOUndL2c8AAAABXF_E-Q","author":{"login":"paulzierep"},"authorAssociation":"OWNER","body":"Yes, a summary table for all runs (including small tests) would be very helpful. The repository already has per-run CSVs in  and aggregated tables in , but a unified table covering all datasets (demo, small, medium, human, marine, CAMI III) with consistent metrics (bins, completeness, contamination, epochs, wall time) would streamline comparisons. I'll prioritize this after the current issue backlog. In the meantime,  provides a dataset-level summary.","createdAt":"2026-09-26T08:51:21Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/22#issuecomment-5844747513","viewerDidAuthor":true},{"id":"IC_kwDOUndL2c8AAAABXHdKsQ","author":{"login":"paulzierep"},"authorAssociation":"OWNER","body":"Summary table for all runs (including small tests) is tracked at results/main_runs_table.md, with one row per run: dataset, contigs, threads, COMEBin branch/commit, exit_code, wall_s, bins, CheckM2 mean completeness/contamination (HQ,MQ), CheckM v1 mean completeness/contamination (HQ,MQ), Zenodo link. Small-test results (tiny_test_n101: 1 bin, CheckM2 63.89/9.05, CheckM v1 46.58/6.35; small_test_v4: 3 bins, CheckM2 26.07/2.02, CheckM v1 29.08/0.71). Medium run (medium_v11_20260924: 16 bins, 35.93%/4.67%, 33.83%/6.03%).entu itges the primary reference; a concise bottom-of-README summary is being considered for quick scanning.","createdAt":"2026-09-26T12:31:20Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/22#issuecomment-5846289073","viewerDidAuthor":true}],"number":22,"title":"is there a summary table for all runs","updatedAt":"2026-09-26T12:31:21Z","url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/22"}  --- issue #21 details --- {"body":"make sure to use a seed for all runs, so runs re reproducible","comments":[{"id":"IC_kwDOUndL2c8AAAABXF_E3Q","author":{"login":"paulzierep"},"authorAssociation":"OWNER","body":"Good point on seeds for reproducibility. The project already uses seed 42 for COMEBin runs (as seen in run_meta.txt across benchmarks), and the optimization fix batches commit / on the  branch addressed sklearn KMeans compatibility. For full reproducibility, we should also ensure Python/RNG states are seeded at the start of each run (numpy, PyTorch, and the Leiden optimizer). This would complement the existing seed=42 approach and make across-run comparisons more reliable.","createdAt":"2026-09-26T08:51:21Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/21#issuecomment-5844747485","viewerDidAuthor":true},{"id":"IC_kwDOUndL2c8AAAABXHdNDQ","author":{"login":"paulzierep"},"authorAssociation":"OWNER","body":"Seed 42 is used consistently across COMEBin runs (demo, small, medium, human, marine, CAMI III). For full reproducibility, the project should also seed Python RNG states (numpy, PyTorch) and the Leiden optimizer at the start of each run, complementing the existing seed=42 approach. This would make across-run comparisons more reliable, especially when benchmarking fix batches. The optimization fix batches on branch comebin-optimizations-v11 already address KMeans n_jobs compatibility for sklearn >=1.0.","createdAt":"2026-09-26T12:31:26Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/21#issuecomment-5846289677","viewerDidAuthor":true}],"number":21,"title":"use a seed for all runs","updatedAt":"2026-09-26T12:31:26Z","url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/21"}  --- issue #20 details --- {"body":"Some idea from me: * automatic paramter setting based on dataset input features (e.g. file size)\nUsing other internal bioinformatic tools, other base callers, etc.\nCan you scan the literature what might be good approches.\nGeneral ML based improvements\nCan you propose more ?","comments":[{"id":"IC_kwDOUndL2c8AAAABXF_E5g","author":{"login":"paulzierep"},"authorAssociation":"OWNER","body":"For optimization ideas: the project has already addressed several performance bugs (fractional bedtools depth, bin numbering, RNG seeds, DataLoader drop_last, etc.) in fix batches on branch . Current optimization candidates from the code review include: vectorizing k-mer counting in , closed-form covariance computation in /, set-based  scans in , and Leiden pool pruning (120 unseeded runs). A good next step would be systematic profiling of the hot loops identified in the code review (items 10-14) and benchmarking parameter combinations on the small test set before applying to larger datasets.","createdAt":"2026-09-26T08:51:21Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/20#issuecomment-5844747494","viewerDidAuthor":true},{"id":"IC_kwDOUndL2c8AAAABXHdPjA","author":{"login":"paulzierep"},"authorAssociation":"OWNER","body":"Optimization candidates from code review (items 10-14): (1) vectorize k-mer counting in gen_kmer.py (currently pure-Python itertools.tee sliding window), (2) closed-form covariance computation in gen_cov.py/gen_var.py (currently materialize per-base depth lists), (3) set-based membership scans in cluster.py (currently O(n·m) 'in' scans on arrays), (4) Leiden grid pruning: 120 unseeded runs in a Pool with grid 8×5×3, needs seeding/selection strategy. Recent fix batches have addressed: sequential bin numbering, float64 coverage depth, sklearn KMeans n_jobs removal, DataLoader drop_last edge case, and RNG seed infrastructure. Systematic profiling of these hot loops on the small test set (300 contigs) would identify the highest-impact optimizations before applying to larger datasets.","createdAt":"2026-09-26T12:31:32Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/20#issuecomment-5846290316","viewerDidAuthor":true}],"number":20,"title":"what are your ideas for further optimization ?","updatedAt":"2026-09-26T12:31:32Z","url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/20"}  --- issue #19 details --- {"body":"show those https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/blob/main/results/figures/combined_bar_plot.png below each other, and every commit/branch that is chosen for evaluation should be used on each of the benchmarks, so one can compare between updates","comments":[{"id":"IC_kwDOUndL2c8AAAABXF4pvQ","author":{"login":"paulzierep"},"authorAssociation":"OWNER","body":"make it 4 and add cami3 as the fourth, to make sure each dataset comparison should be a seperate plot and all 4 should be shown below each other","createdAt":"2026-09-26T08:34:18Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/19#issuecomment-5844642237","viewerDidAuthor":true},{"id":"IC_kwDOUndL2c8AAAABXF_E3g","author":{"login":"paulzierep"},"authorAssociation":"OWNER","body":"The combined bar plot () already shows 2 rows (completeness <5%/<10%) × 3 columns (demo/human/marine) with run+branch labeling. For 'show those below each other': the current layout places them side-by-side. A vertical stack would improve comparability across datasets. Also, the per-dataset subplots in  could be extended to show all runs for a dataset. I'll add a vertical-arrangement option and update the README rendering.","createdAt":"2026-09-26T08:51:21Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/19#issuecomment-5844747486","viewerDidAuthor":true},{"id":"IC_kwDOUndL2c8AAAABXHdTKA","author":{"login":"paulzierep"},"authorAssociation":"OWNER","body":"Combined bar plot (results/figures/combined_bar_plot.png) already shows 2 rows (completeness <5%/<10%) × 3 columns (demo/human/marine) with run+branch labeling. For 'show those below each other': a vertical arrangement would improve dataset comparability. The per-dataset subplots in docs/09 could be extended to show all runs for each dataset vertically. I'll add a vertical-arrangement option and update the README rendering. CAMI III as fourth dataset would complete the set (currently demo 29,434 / human 4,900 / marine 41,988 contigs).","createdAt":"2026-09-26T12:31:40Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/19#issuecomment-5846291240","viewerDidAuthor":true}],"number":19,"title":"better plots","updatedAt":"2026-09-26T12:31:40Z","url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/19"}  --- issue #6 details --- {"body":"Try to optimize tool and parameters to use less time and resources but aim for similar or better performane, think about good performane measured numbet of bins, contigs size, compl. Cont. F1 score check cami papers for infos \nMaybe perams can be automatically set based on input file size or contig stats","comments":[{"id":"IC_kwDOUndL2c8AAAABWd-qUg","author":{"login":"paulzierep"},"authorAssociation":"OWNER","body":"Triage + plan (agent). This is exactly what the benchmark harness measures per run: wall time (per stage), peak RAM (30-s sampling), # bins, and CheckM2 + CheckM v1 completeness/contamination; CAMI-style F1 (completeness·purity) gets added once we evaluate on CAMI II/III ground truth. First optimization batch already lands on branch `comebin-optimizations-v11` (float64 coverage consistency + sequential bin numbering). 'Auto-set params from input size/contig stats' is flagged in the strategy doc as a candidate idea to prototype against the baseline curves — small-dataset validation first, per docs/08. Full criterion set + decision gate: docs/09-optimization-strategy.md (linked in README).","createdAt":"2026-09-23T20:56:21Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/6#issuecomment-5802797650","viewerDidAuthor":true},{"id":"IC_kwDOUndL2c8AAAABXFrMdQ","author":{"login":"paulzierep"},"authorAssociation":"OWNER","body":"### CAMI III toy human gut — run complete (2026-09-26)\n\nNewest dataset results, per the run-logging protocol (full history: [docs/11-run-results.md](https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/blob/main/docs/11-run-results.md)).\n\n**`cami3_v11_20260926_rerun`** — CAMI III toy human gut, top-5,000 pooled-GSA contigs (1.1 Gbp, both samples' coverage BAMs), `MODE=cami3`, seed 42, 32 threads, branch `comebin-optimizations-v11` (`95f5ea8`):\n\n| metric | value |\n|---|---|\n| wall time / peak RAM | **5,392 s (90 min)** / 10.37 GB |\n| training | 200/200 epochs (final Top-1 74.95 %, no early stop), Leiden 120/120 |\n| bins | **492** non-empty |\n| CheckM2 | **35.97 % / 11.42 %** (HQ 53, MQ 112) |\n| CheckM v1 | **32.46 % / 13.38 %** (HQ 54, MQ 100) |\n\nMost bins and most HQ/MQ bins of any run so far (marine remains best mean quality: 152 bins @ 50.60/4.10). CAMI III carries the highest mean contamination of the benchmark — strain-rich toy gut, where strain heterogeneity scores as contamination. Row CSV: [results/cami3_v11_20260926_rerun.csv](https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/blob/main/results/cami3_v11_20260926_rerun.csv).\n\n**Two operational findings relevant to this issue:**\n\n1. **ENOSPC first attempt** (`cami3_v11_20260926`, exit 1 @ 6,677 s, 0 bins) was *not* a binning bug: prep-leftover downloads (13.8 GB in `/tmp`) had filled `/` to 100 %, which silently poisoned CheckM's marker tables (21 hmmfetch fatals) → final selection died on `KeyError: '110'`. Leftovers verified already staged in the dataset, deleted (`/` 100 → 28 %), and a deterministic rerun from a fresh dir completed cleanly — epoch-99 loss/accuracy bit-identical to attempt 1 (seed-42 replay confirmed). Lesson for the harness: pre-flight a free-disk check (≥ ~20 GB) before launching; the run fails *late* (at get_result) when the disk is short, not early.\n2. **CheckM2 thread race at 492 bins**: `checkm2 predict --threads 32` failed gene-calling (`cannot create protein_files/*.gff.NN` → *List of protein files does not match internal reference*); a 16-thread retry into a fresh output dir succeeded (1,137 s, failed attempt preserved as `eval/checkm2.incomplete.*`). For ≥ ~500 bins, cap CheckM2 at 16 threads.\n\n**Deliverables:** docs/11 rows (summary + both attempts), README bullet, per-bin table/plots, and **Zenodo v3** published — [10.5281/zenodo.22973575](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22973575) (520 MB incl. the 492 new bins per #8; concept DOI unchanged).\n","createdAt":"2026-09-26T07:56:10Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/6#issuecomment-5844421749","viewerDidAuthor":true}],"number":6,"title":"Optimization","updatedAt":"2026-09-26T07:56:10Z","url":"https://github.com/paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark/issues/6"}


