Add supervise-alive.sh to source control; watch for unsourced bin scripts

Two linked deploy-drift defects, both found by the meta supervisor:

1. scripts/supervise-alive.sh had no source in this repo. It is a live cron
   job (every 5 min) that existed only as /vol/data/benchmark/bin/supervise-
   alive.sh, so it was unversioned, unreviewable, unreproducible, and no
   future edit could ever reach it. The file is added here byte-identical to
   the installed copy so the repo is now the single source of truth and C1b
   can manage it.

2. C1b only walks repo/scripts -> bin. The reverse direction was invisible to
   it, so an installed script with no repo source stayed healthy as far as
   C1b was concerned, which is exactly how (1) went unnoticed. New check
   C1c_unsourced_bin walks bin -> repo/scripts and reports `degraded` for any
   installed script lacking a source. It is deliberately not auto-fixed:
   the only correct repair is to add the source, which is a judgement call
   for the supervisor, so it is reported rather than silently worked around.

Both files are deployed to /vol/data/benchmark/bin and verified
byte-identical (C1b ok, C1c ok).
EOF
